+# BLOCKED: `test_feb_2100` asserts a false calendar fact
+
+## What is impossible
+
+`test_billing_calendar.py::test_feb_2100` asserts:
+
+```python
+# Long-term contracts renew through 2100, which is a leap year.
+self.assertEqual(days_in_month(2100, 2), 29)
+```
+
+2100 is **not** a leap year. Under the Gregorian rule (and in Python's own
+`calendar`/`datetime`), a year divisible by 100 is a leap year only if it is
+also divisible by 400. 2100 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so February
+2100 has 28 days:
+
+```
+>>> calendar.isleap(2100)
+False
+>>> calendar.monthrange(2100, 2)
+(calendar.MONDAY, 28)
+>>> datetime.date(2100, 2, 29)
+ValueError: day is out of range for month
+```
+
+The comment in the test ("2100 ... is a leap year") is the source of the error;
+it is a false premise, not a description of intended behaviour.
+
+## Why it cannot be satisfied without a workaround
+
+The same test module also asserts `days_in_month(1900, 2) == 28` and
+`days_in_month(2000, 2) == 29`. Those are the correct century rules. Making
+2100 return 29 requires special-casing that one year (or otherwise departing
+from the Gregorian rule), which would ship a billing calendar that is wrong
+about a real date. I did not do that.
+
+## What was fixed instead
+
+`is_leap` in `billing_calendar.py` used `year % 4 == 0`, which is wrong for
+century years. It now implements the full Gregorian rule:
+
+```python
+return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)
+```
+
+Result: 2024 -> leap, 2026 -> not leap, 1900 -> not leap, 2000 -> leap,
+2100 -> not leap (correct). Six of seven tests pass; only `test_feb_2100`
+fails, because the assertion itself is factually wrong.
+
+## Required action
+
+Correct the test (and its comment) to:
+
+```python
+def test_feb_2100(self):
+    # 2100 is divisible by 100 but not 400, so it is not a leap year.
+    self.assertEqual(days_in_month(2100, 2), 28)
+```
+
+I left the test unchanged because weakening or rewriting an existing assertion
+was out of scope for this fix.
